a particular group of people."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The dialects of the same language are mainly distinguished from each other by differences in linguistic features such as phonology, morphology, syntax and vocabulary."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The term \"dialect\" is most often used to refer to regional dialects."}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'wikipedia-cc-by-sa-4', 'Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Wikipedia, "Dialect", CC BY-SA 4.0, retrieved 2026-11-02. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dialect', FALSE, 'active') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET external_id = EXCLUDED.external_id, assessments = EXCLUDED.assessments, section = EXCLUDED.section, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, skill = EXCLUDED.skill, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, format = EXCLUDED.format, stimulus = EXCLUDED.stimulus, prompt = EXCLUDED.prompt, choices = EXCLUDED.choices, figure_metadata = EXCLUDED.figure_metadata, source_id = EXCLUDED.source_id, source_name = EXCLUDED.source_name, license = EXCLUDED.license, attribution = EXCLUDED.attribution, is_released_practice_set = EXCLUDED.is_released_practice_set, status = 'active';
INSERT INTO public.question_keys (question_id, correct_answer, acceptable_answers, explanation, distractor_rationales) VALUES ('RW-M32-CE009', 'C', ARRAY['C']::TEXT[], 'The correct quotation lists phonology, morphology, syntax, and vocabulary as distinguishing features.', '{"B": "This sentence defines a dialect.", "D": "This sentence describes regional dialects.", "A": "This sentence defines an idiolect."}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT (question_id) DO UPDATE SET correct_answer = EXCLUDED.correct_answer, acceptable_answers = EXCLUDED.acceptable_answers, explanation = EXCLUDED.explanation, distractor_rationales = EXCLUDED.distractor_rationales;
INSERT INTO public.questions (id, external_id, assessments, section, domain, skill, difficulty, format, stimulus, prompt, choices, figure_metadata, source_id, source_name, license, attribution, is_released_practice_set, status) VALUES ('RW-M32-CI010', 'HDSAT-RW-M32-CI010', ARRAY['SAT', 'PSAT/NMSQT', 'PSAT 10', 'PSAT 8/9']::TEXT[], 'Reading and Writing', 'Information and Ideas', 'Central Ideas and Details', 'Medium', 'multiple_choice', 'In linguistics, code-switching or language alternation is the process of shifting from one linguistic code (a language or dialect) to another, depending on the social context or conversational setting. These alternations are generally intended to influence the relationship between the speakers, for example, suggesting that they may share identities based on similar linguistic histories. Code-switching is different from plurilingualism in that plurilingualism refers to the ability of an individual to use multiple languages, while code-switching is the act of using multiple languages together. Multilinguals (speakers of more than one language) sometimes use elements of multiple languages when conversing with each other. Thus, code-switching is the use of more than one linguistic variety in a manner consistent with the syntax and phonology of each variety. Code-switching may happen between sentences, sentence fragments, words, or individual morphemes (in synthetic languages). However, some linguists consider the borrowing of words or morphemes from another language to be different from other types of code-switching. Code-switching can occur when there is a change in the environment in which one is speaking, or in the context of speaking a different language or switching the verbiage to match that of the audience.', 'Which choice best states the main idea of the text?', '[{"id": "A", "text": "Code-switching is unrelated to social context."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Code-switching is shifting between languages or dialects according to social context, distinct from simply knowing multiple languages."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Code-switching means translating written texts."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Code-switching is the ability to speak only one language."}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'wikipedia-cc-by-sa-4', 'Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Wikipedia, "Code-switching", CC BY-SA 4.0, retrieved 2026-11-02. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Code-switching', FALSE, 'active') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET external_id = EXCLUDED.external_id, assessments = EXCLUDED.assessments, section = EXCLUDED.section, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, skill = EXCLUDED.skill, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, format = EXCLUDED.format, stimulus = EXCLUDED.stimulus, prompt = EXCLUDED.prompt, choices = EXCLUDED.choices, figure_metadata = EXCLUDED.figure_metadata, source_id = EXCLUDED.source_id, source_name = EXCLUDED.source_name, license = EXCLUDED.license, attribution = EXCLUDED.attribution, is_released_practice_set = EXCLUDED.is_released_practice_set, status = 'active';
INSERT INTO public.question_keys (question_id, correct_answer, acceptable_answers, explanation, distractor_rationales) VALUES ('RW-M32-CI010', 'B', ARRAY['B']::TEXT[], 'The passage defines code-switching, distinguishes it from plurilingualism, and describes where it occurs.', '{"D": "The text defines it as shifting from one linguistic code to another depending on context.", "C": "The text says plurilingualism is the ability to use multiple languages.", "A": "The text distinguishes translation from code-switching."}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT (question_id) DO UPDATE SET correct_answer = EXCLUDED.correct_answer, acceptable_answers = EXCLUDED.acceptable_answers, explanation = EXCLUDED.explanation, distractor_rationales = EXCLUDED.distractor_rationales;
INSERT INTO public.questions (id, external_id, assessments, section, domain, skill, difficulty, format, stimulus, prompt, choices, figure_metadata, source_id, source_name, license, attribution, is_released_practice_set, status) VALUES ('RW-M32-INF010', 'HDSAT-RW-M32-INF010', ARRAY['SAT', 'PSAT/NMSQT', 'PSAT 10', 'PSAT 8/9']::TEXT[], 'Reading and Writing', 'Information and Ideas', 'Inferences', 'Medium', 'multiple_choice', 'In linguistics, code-switching or language alternation is the process of shifting from one linguistic code (a language or dialect) to another, depending on the social context or conversational setting. These alternations are generally intended to influence the relationship between the speakers, for example, suggesting that they may share identities based on similar linguistic histories. Code-switching is different from plurilingualism in that plurilingualism refers to the ability of an individual to use multiple languages, while code-switching is the act of using multiple languages together. Multilinguals (speakers of more than one language) sometimes use elements of multiple languages when conversing with each other. Thus, code-switching is the use of more than one linguistic variety in a manner consistent with the syntax and phonology of each variety. Code-switching may happen between sentences, sentence fragments, words, or individual morphemes (in synthetic languages). However, some linguists consider the borrowing of words or morphemes from another language to be different from other types of code-switching. Code-switching can occur when there is a change in the environment in which one is speaking, or in the context of speaking a different language or switching the verbiage to match that of the audience.
INSERT INTO public.question_keys (question_id, correct_answer, acceptable_answers, explanation, distractor_rationales) VALUES ('RW-M32-INF010', 'D', ARRAY['D']::TEXT[], 'Using another code can signal common identity or fit a social setting.', '{"A": "The text says alternations suggest speakers may share identities based on linguistic histories.", "C": "The text describes a social purpose.", "B": "Code-switching is described as ordinary multilingual conversation."}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT (question_id) DO UPDATE SET correct_answer = EXCLUDED.correct_answer, acceptable_answers = EXCLUDED.acceptable_answers, explanation = EXCLUDED.explanation, distractor_rationales = EXCLUDED.distractor_rationales;
